    "image_with_text_WrPkf9": {
      "type": "image-with-text",
      "blocks": {
        "heading_4h7hUy": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading_text": "Keep soil chemistry in the truffle “sweet spot”",
            "heading_size": "h3",
            "enable_h1_tag": false
          }
        },
        "description_aXcfz8": {
          "type": "description",
          "settings": {
            "description_text": "<p>Périgord truffles require loose, free-draining soil with a relatively high pH and adequate active limestone and calcium. At these high pH levels, several nutrients become less available to the tree and the truffle fungus, so simply “being alkaline” is not enough—you also need to know which elements are drifting out of range.</p>"
          }
        },
        "subheading_87cFFg": {
          "type": "subheading",
          "settings": {
            "subheading_text": "Annual soil analysis helps you:"
          }
        },
        "description_jTPee9": {
          "type": "description",
          "settings": {
            "description_text": "<ul><li>Track pH and a host of other nutrient and trace element levels as they respond to irrigation, liming, and natural processes. </li><li>Identify emerging nutrient deficiencies or excesses that could stress trees or the truffle fungus. </li><li>Adjust lime and amendment programs before problems become severe or expensive to correct.</li></ul><p>Without this feedback, it is easy for soils to drift slowly away from the narrow chemical window in which <em>Tuber melanosporum</em> can thrive.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "heading_4h7hUy",
        "description_aXcfz8",
        "subheading_87cFFg",
        "description_jTPee9"
      ],
